<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shengle</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shengle was the early centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuoba">Tuoba</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a>, near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horinger">Horinger</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inner-mongolia">Inner Mongolia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Tradition says the Tuoba leader Liwei settled there in 258, and it served as a capital of the state of Dai and of the early Northern Wei before the court moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/datong">Pingcheng</a> (Datong) in 398. Its walled site has been excavated.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Shengle lay south of the Yin Mountains and north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-wall-of-china">Great Wall of China</a>, in country long held by nomadic peoples. Before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> it was dominated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>; most of them moved west, allowing the Xianbei and Wuhuan to migrate into the area, though some Xiongnu stayed. At the time of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuoba">Tuoba</a>'s arrival it belonged to Chengle County in the Dingxiang Commandery, which was abandoned during the 3rd century because of Xiongnu invasions. The economy of the Tuoba centre was chiefly nomadic, although farming was also practised.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Tuoba Liwei, the first leader of the Tuoba clan, encouraged his people to move to Shengle in the mid-3rd century, and from there the clan rose to political prominence. In 312 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuoba-yilu">Tuoba Yilu</a>, the first ruler of Dai, made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/datong">Pingcheng</a> a southern capital and left Shengle as the northern one, but after his death in 316 the court gave up Pingcheng and Shengle again became the sole centre.</p><p>When the Northern Wei was founded in 386, after the fall of Dai, it kept Shengle as its capital. As it conquered land to the south from the Later Yan, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuoba">Tuoba</a> shifted their weight southwards and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/datong">Pingcheng</a> grew in importance, until in 398, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-daowu-of-northern-wei">Tuoba Gui</a>, the capital was moved there. The Chinese historian Fengwen Peng argues that Shengle combined political and cultural elements of the steppe peoples and of the Central Plains, and that the move to Pingcheng marks the Northern Wei's change from a nomadic power to a dynasty in the Central Plains tradition. The site later became the seat of Zhenwu County under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao dynasty</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The ruins lie at Xiyaozi and Tuchengzi villages in the town of Shengle, Horinger County, about 40 kilometres south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohhot">Hohhot</a>. They were unearthed in 1971, and parts of the rammed-earth walls survive, the outer circuit running 1,550 metres east to west and 2,250 metres north to south. Relics from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a> have also been found there.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shengle” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 424 the khagan Datan raided the old Wei centre of <b>Shengle</b> with a large cavalry army, but Wei armies campaigned deep into Mongolia repeatedly, and Emperor Taiwu's great expedition of 429 surprised the Rouran, forcing Datan to flee and capturing large numbers of people and animals.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rouran-khaganate">Rouran Khaganate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>His centre was <b>Shengle</b>, near modern Horinger in Inner Mongolia.</blockquote><a href="/state/dai-sixteen-kingdoms">Dai (Sixteen Kingdoms)</a></li></ul></section>
